even for a country long gripped by  violence it was a shocking crime on the  morning of November 23 2009 a convoy of  vehicles carrying journalists and  members of a political family were on  their way to an electoral event in  Maguindanao province in the southern  Philippines they never reached their  destination a gang of armed men stopped  and forced them to a hilltop in the  village of Messala there they were  repeatedly shot 58 people were killed  over a period of hours and buried in a  mass grave 32 or journalists others were  members of the Maldonado family  political rivals to the powerful  impeccable plan two key witnesses  interviewed exclusively by Al Jazeera  said the armed men were working for the  late governor and Ilan Padawan and sons  and that they witnessed how the family  planned and executed the victims  since then two governments have come and  gone and there have been no convictions  the man with the data family decided to  take their case to the courts but the  case has been difficult from the  beginning more than 200 people are  accused more than a hundred are in jail  and at least 80 at large  now Supreme Court officials see verdicts  are expected this month this memorial  was built on the same hills where the  victims were murdered the Maguindanao  massacre was one of the country's worst  cases of political violence and the  single deadliest attack against  journalists in global history the  families of those who died say fighting  for justice has been difficult and  painful we expect nothing more than a  full conviction for the most barbaric  inhuman act they have committed we  waited for long and suffered so much the  families say for years they've felt  abandoned by the government and the  public they say has become apathetic in  the case but they remain determined  fighting not just for justice but to be  remembered and ensure their country will  not forget the brutality of what  happened 10 years ago in the village of  Messala Jamela Alan Logan al-jazeera  Maguindanao province southern  Philippines
 

